Z||000004||theogre||04-26-99||07:36 PM||||for reference.... many places no longer cary chiltons. Pep Boys even mostly caries Haynes books.<p>Another good source for books about auto systems is Petersons. These books cover a system, not a specific car. you will understand AC, for example, much better with a Petersons book, than a haynes car manual.
Z||000005||ezsurf||04-26-99||09:17 PM||||EMF,<br>I have the original Pontiac shop service manual for the 86 Fiero.  Dealer service manuals are far more detailed than Haynes or Chiltons, but they are expensive.  It cost me $81 to have it delivered to my door.  It is published by Helm publications, no book store can get it.  Not even Amazon.com. (Talk about a monopoly.)  I frist tried to order it through a Pontiac dealer, they can't get them either.  You can reach Helm publications at 1-800-551-4123.  I had to order it "sight unseen".  So, I asked if you can return it.  You can return within 30 days, but there is a 25% restocking fee.  So, make sure you order the correct year.  I've dealt with other shop manuals on other vehicles and found that the original service manual is the only way to go.  Incidently, I heard that Haynes bought Chiltons. So, Chiltons won't be around anymore. <br>
Z||000006||EMF||04-26-99||09:50 PM||||Thanks. <p>chihltons does suck, in the book i have the whole wiring  and electrical system is in text, like 2 pictures, hehe.<br>
